# Ventricular and hypothalamic landmark reasoning — draft revision 1

Three original Apply anatomy concepts distinguish CSF-producing choroid plexus,
the posterior hypothalamic mammillary landmarks and the anterior lamina
terminalis. They reuse existing practice controls and three existing displayed
root selections. The parent source is
`ed3c8cc0e99f6cf850a915d7a08de2e6489c1b6f`; no geometry, tissue or image is added.

| Target | Exact existing source | Scope that must remain visible |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Choroid plexus of cerebral hemisphere | FMA61934; FJ1755, FJ1803 | One midline-tagged bilateral group, not all ventricular plexuses or a secretion/flow simulation |
| Mammillary body | FMA74877; FJ1768, FJ1815 | Paired anatomy represented by one grouped selection, not separately labelled sides or resolved nuclei |
| Lamina terminalis | FMA61975; FJ1764, FJ1812 | Source files touch at one vertex with separate normals; not a reconstructed continuous membrane or a safe procedural boundary |

All three exact complete displayed identities and their two unchanged bundles
are pinned in `content/ventricular-landmark-reasoning-pins.json`. A broad name,
FMA-only, side-only or file-only match cannot admit a different source selection.
The laterality tag is a source-selection property, not a claim that paired
anatomy is anatomically unpaired. No identity hold is removed.

## References, original authorship and rights

Factual references inspected 23 October 2026:

- [UTHealth Neuroanatomy Online: ventricles](https://nba.uth.tmc.edu/neuroanatomy/L4/Lab04p01_index.html): ventricular choroid plexus and the anterior third-ventricular wall.
- [UTHealth Neuroanatomy Online: hypothalamic landmarks](https://nba.uth.tmc.edu/neuroanatomy/L11/Lab11p14_index.html): anterior lamina terminalis and posterior mammillary region.

Questions and explanations are newly written project prose. University pages
are factual reading references, not licences to redistribute their text, tables,
question banks, figures, images or models; none is copied. No new third-party
asset, texture, font, dependency, fee or required service is introduced. Original
code/prose retain project MIT terms; existing BodyParts3D CC BY 4.0 attribution,
source notices and model restrictions remain unchanged.

## Delivery and clinical review

The three questions remain editorial drafts requiring fresh revision-bound
radiologist review. The actual practice engine supplies the worksheet snapshot:
prompt, correct source identity, eligible same-tag alternatives, explanation and
citations. It must not silently retain an older teaching approval. Existing
geometry/imaging reviews are separate and their material must remain unchanged.
All preceding 190 concept definitions/order and 343 previously bound selections
remain exact. New totals are 193 concepts and 346 represented selections, not
193 validated assessment items. All 9,936 nine-topic lesson placements remain
unchanged; reasoning is additional worksheet material, not a readiness promotion.

These concepts support learning usual spatial relationships relevant to later
CT/MRI comparison. They do not establish acquired-image visibility, masks,
patient-space registration, fluid dynamics, diagnosis or a surgical route.
No source scan, accepted mask, clinical PACS, private decision or entitlement is
edited. The installed Website/preview remains unchanged; generated learner and
protected Review integration requires its own later verified candidate.
